#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# eval

#% text
# `eval(e)` gives the value of `e`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Outside a held argument `eval` changes nothing: its argument is evaluated like any other, and `eval(e)` is that value.

#% item
# Written directly as an argument that a function holds, `eval(e)` forces that argument: `e` is evaluated before the call, and the function receives its value. The function's other held arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# On the right of a definition, `eval` is the identity too: `rule g(x_) := eval(x^2 + 1)` behaves as `rule g(x_) := x^2 + 1`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
eval(1 + 1)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
rule g(x_) := eval(x^2 + 1)
g(3)
#% output
#= 10

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A function that holds all its arguments receives `1 + 1` as written, and the value of an argument wrapped in `eval`:

#% cell
set_attributes(h, hold_all); [h(1 + 1), h(eval(1 + 1))]
#% output
#= [h(1 + 1), h(2)]

#% text
# `quote` holds its argument too, so `eval` inside it is evaluated first:

#% cell
quote(eval(1 + 1)) === quote(2)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `eval` of a value is that value:

#% cell
eval([1, x, "s"]) === [1, x, "s"]
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `attributes`, `set_attributes`
